{
  "_about": "Every kind of document the firm holds (docs/design_plan.md Part 1 item 2): its name on screen, the exhibit roles it can fill, whether it has an expiry, whether it is confidential (8 U.S.C. 1367: VAWA, T and U; 8 CFR 208.6: asylum), who says whose it is ('document': the reader names the person; 'reviewer': a person sets it), and whether it is read beyond its type. 'recognized' says how the classifier knows it: 'text' (src/classify/patterns.py, which reads the names from here and keeps its own patterns), 'explicit_header' (title plus table), 'form_footer' and 'notice_case_type' (src/classify/classifier.py), 'browser_header', 'fallback', or 'not_yet' (named in the packets; a paralegal places it until a real sample confirms a reader). src/documents.py builds each case's documents.json from it. tests/test_documents.py keeps it and the classifier in step. 'language' is the language every document of the type is printed in (English for a U.S. government document); 'language_by': 'country' says the language follows the issuing country (schemas/country_languages.json) when the document's own words don't say; 'has_dates': false marks a kind of document that carries no date (a Social Security card). 'role_uses' says what each role means and where a packet uses it. 'short_name' is the name staff screens show in a column when 'name' is longer than 40 characters (the long name stays on hover, in the review bundle and in the classifier, which never reads 'short_name'; tests/test_document_names.py).",
  "roles": [
    "identity",
    "nationality",
    "status",
    "entry",
    "relationship",
    "presence",
    "good_faith",
    "hardship",
    "court_record",
    "notice",
    "medical",
    "translation",
    "correspondence",
    "photo",
    "other"
  ],
  "role_uses": {
    "identity": "Who the person is (a photo identity document or a civil record of the name and birth). Packets place these documents by their type.",
    "nationality": "The person's nationality or citizenship (a passport, a national identity card, a U.S. passport or naturalization certificate). No exhibit is placed by this role alone: the packet places these documents by their type (the passport, identity, petitioner's status and relative exhibits).",
    "status": "An immigration status the document grants or records (a green card, a refugee approval). No exhibit is placed by this role alone: the packet places these documents by their type.",
    "entry": "An entry to the U.S. (an I-94, an admission stamp, a U.S. visa, an advance parole document used to return). An exhibit named 'entry' takes a document tagged with it.",
    "relationship": "A family relationship (a birth, marriage or divorce record; an approved relative petition). An exhibit named 'relationship' takes a document tagged with it.",
    "presence": "Living in the U.S. over time (leases, bills, pay, school). An exhibit named 'presence' takes a document tagged with it, and an exhibit that lists 'presence' under its roles takes these documents by their type.",
    "good_faith": "A real marriage (joint leases, accounts, tax returns, photos together). The exhibit named 'good_faith' takes a document tagged with it.",
    "hardship": "Hardship to the client or a relative. The exhibit named 'hardship' takes a document tagged with it.",
    "court_record": "A court or police record. Packets place these documents by their type.",
    "notice": "A government notice (USCIS, the court, the consulate). Packets place these documents by their type.",
    "medical": "A medical record or exam. Packets place these documents by their type.",
    "translation": "A translation or the translator's certificate: it goes beside the document it translates.",
    "correspondence": "Letters and forms about the representation. Never an exhibit by this role.",
    "photo": "A photograph. A person says what it shows with another role.",
    "other": "Anything else. A person chooses its exhibit."
  },
  "people": [
